<p>The National Savings Certificate (NSC) is a government-backed 5-year fixed-income instrument issued through post offices and authorised banks. The interest rate is set quarterly by the Ministry of Finance — approximately 7.7% p.a. as of Q1 FY 2026-27 (<strong>verify the current quarter's rate at nsiindia.gov.in before investing</strong>, as small-savings rates are revised quarterly). NSC has no maximum investment limit, a minimum purchase of ₹1,000, and qualifies for Section 80C deduction on the principal invested under the old tax regime (within the combined ₹1.5 lakh annual 80C cap). Unlike bank FDs, NSC does not pay interest periodically — interest accrues annually but is paid entirely as a lump sum at maturity alongside the principal, making it a pure wealth-accumulation instrument rather than a regular income tool.</p>

<p>NSC interest has a tax treatment that most investors misunderstand. Interest credited in Years 1 through 4 is treated as deemed reinvested in NSC — so it qualifies as a fresh NSC investment eligible for Section 80C deduction in those years, within the ₹1.5L cap. Only the Year 5 interest — the final year's accrual paid out at maturity — is taxable as "income from other sources" in the year the NSC matures. In practice: on ₹1 lakh at 7.7%, Year 1 interest is ₹7,700; this is both "deemed received" and "deemed reinvested," letting you include ₹7,700 as an additional 80C deduction in Year 2 on top of any new NSC purchases. NSC certificates can also be pledged as collateral for bank loans or transferred to another individual (subject to post-office procedures). Use the <a href="/india/section-80c-calculator/">Section 80C Calculator</a> to plan how NSC fits within your total 80C allocation.</p>
